															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mr-lapin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5968622); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5968622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607158693" alt="Mr Lapin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5968622)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mr-lapin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5968622); return false;" >Mr Lapin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607141178 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Dec 2020 04:06</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4836618">
									

<p>I wont deny that I've recently become kind of enamored with the UIU myself, which is a big part of why I thought this would be fun. To address your questions, though -</p>
<p>As to the character interactions (which, I agree, are going to be what sells the story even moreso than my attempts at writing mystery or action). To that end, I've been kind of working out my thoughts for the team.</p>
<p>So far the character clearest in my mind is &quot;Droopy&quot; Drummond, the team leader, who earned the nickname due to an outlook on the edge between 'realistic' and 'pessimistic' after his experiences in the UIU (&quot;We have excellent bereavement benefits&quot; and all that). In fact, in my head, his first question when asked to investigate this scenario is &quot;Shouldn't we be passing this on to the Skippers?&quot;</p>
<p>With a guy like that, I'll probably need a more outgoing and eager sort to balance him out (I'm thinking at least one &quot;rookie&quot; recently transferred to the UIU who has an outlook that there's nothing that enough effort and/or bullets can't deal with).</p>
<p>The remainder of the group&#8230; well, probably following the classic Four Temperaments wouldn't be a bad idea. I will admit that, as it is, for the group as a whole I was thinking of some fairly pulp-style characters (so &quot;men/women of action&quot; types who may grouse and complain about it and bicker incessantly, but will get things done). The best comparison I could think of would be &quot;Doc Savage's aides without Doc Savage&quot;.<br />
If possible and the story actually survives, I would like to use them as recurring characters for adventures (again &quot;Pulp&quot; so why not make them into a series of weird happenings?)</p>
<p>In the end, I'm a &quot;Good Guys win&quot; sort, so my ending as it stands would be the group does/should succeed and either capture or&#8230; eliminate the Aristocrat infestation. As this would be their first story together, I'd probably use it as the means to have them gain traction together and finally cooperate well enough to do the job.</p>

							</div>

								
					
